      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one keep their house if it's worth more than the asset limit?",
          "answer": "Yes, the primary home is completely exempt from asset limits regardless of value. Only countable assets like bank accounts, investments, and cash value of life insurance over $1,500 count toward the $9,950 (single) or $14,910 (couple) limits."
        },
        {
          "question": "What's the difference between QMB, SLMB, and QI?",
          "answer": "QMB has the lowest income limit ($1,977/month single) but pays all Medicare costs, premiums, deductibles, and copays. SLMB and QI only pay the Medicare Part B premium (about $185/month) but allow higher incomes, up to $2,238/month for SLMB and $2,433/month for QI."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply for my loved one if they have dementia?",
          "answer": "Yes, family members can apply on behalf of elderly loved ones. You'll need to provide all required documentation and may need to establish legal authority like power of attorney for ongoing management."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's income is slightly over the limit?",
          "answer": "Indiana uses higher income limits than federal minimums, so check all three programs. If over QMB limits, try SLMB. If over SLMB, try QI. Income limits update each April, and some deductions may apply to reduce countable income."
        },
        {
          "question": "Will this affect my loved one's Medicare Advantage or Part D coverage?",
          "answer": "No, Medicare Savings Programs work with any Medicare coverage including Medicare Advantage and Part D. QMB members automatically qualify for Medicare Part D Extra Help for prescription costs."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long does QI waitlist typically last?",
          "answer": "QI waitlists vary because funding is limited by federal caps. Apply early in the calendar year for the best chance. QMB and SLMB don't have waitlists, so consider those first if income qualifies."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one have both Medicare Savings Programs and regular Medicaid?",
          "answer": "Yes, through QMB-Also or SLMB-Also categories. These provide Medicare cost assistance plus full Medicaid benefits like Indiana PathWays for Aging if income and assets qualify for both programs."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if a doctor tries to bill my loved one after QMB approval?",
          "answer": "QMB prohibits balance-billing for Medicare-covered services. If providers try to bill your loved one for Medicare deductibles, coinsurance, or copays, report this to Indiana Medicaid immediately. Providers must accept Medicare payment plus QMB coverage as payment in full."
        }
      ],
