      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one work SCSEP and collect Social Security at the same time?",
          "answer": "Yes, but Social Security earnings limits apply if your loved one hasn't reached full retirement age. SCSEP wages count toward the annual limit ($23,400 in 2026 for those under full retirement age). Contact Social Security at (800) 772-1213 to understand how this affects their specific benefits."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if there's no SCSEP opening in our county?",
          "answer": "Indiana has multiple providers serving different regions. Call 2-1-1 to check all available options, including state-administered slots through Indiana DWD. You may need to travel to a neighboring county if that provider has openings and accepts out-of-area residents."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long can my loved one stay in the SCSEP program?",
          "answer": "The goal is transition to unsubsidized employment within about 6 months, though timeframes vary. Participants must recertify eligibility annually and work toward their Individual Employment Plan goals. The program isn't designed for permanent placement."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one gets sick during their SCSEP placement?",
          "answer": "SCSEP provides support services and referrals to help remove barriers to employment, including connecting participants with health resources. Extended absences may require program reassessment, contact your provider immediately to discuss options."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can I apply for SCSEP and unemployment benefits at the same time?",
          "answer": "You must be unemployed to qualify for SCSEP, but once accepted, SCSEP wages may affect unemployment eligibility since you'll be working and earning income. Contact your unemployment office to understand how SCSEP participation impacts benefits."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one's family income is slightly over 125% of poverty level?",
          "answer": "Income limits are firm requirements updated annually. Contact providers directly to verify current thresholds for your household size, as Federal Poverty Guidelines change yearly. There's no flexibility on income limits, but asset limits don't apply."
        },
        {
          "question": "Do SCSEP participants get any benefits besides wages?",
          "answer": "Participants receive minimum wage pay, skills training including computer use, job search assistance, and support service referrals. The program doesn't provide health insurance or other traditional employee benefits, it's designed as temporary paid training toward permanent employment."
        },
        {
          "question": "What types of community service jobs are actually available?",
          "answer": "Common placements include child care aide, customer service, teacher's aide, computer technician, building maintenance, and health care worker roles. Specific opportunities depend on host sites in your area and your skills assessment in the Individual Employment Plan process."
        }
      ],
